  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe proposed approach leverages a genetic algorithm (GA) to globally explore the solution space, addressing the bottleneck of local optimization methods that can become trapped in suboptimal solutions. By using a population of candidate functions, the GA probabilistically evolves better solutions through mutation, crossover, and selection. This method biases exploration toward configurations that spread ones evenly across the interval to minimize overlaps, while maintaining strict feasibility constraints. The GA's global search capability is expected to find lower C\u2085 bounds than previous local methods.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nLet h \u2208 [0, 1]^n_points be a vector representing the step function. The objective is to minimize the maximum overlap integral:\nC\u2085 = max_k [\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx] \nWhich translates to:\nC\u2085 = max_{k=0,...,2n-2} [correlation(h, 1-h)[k] * dx] \nWhere dx = 2.0 / n_points. The constraint \u222bh(x)dx = 1 is equivalent to sum(h) = n_points / 2.\n\nThe GA operates on a population of vectors h \u2208 [0,1]^n_points. Fitness is defined as the computed C\u2085 value. Annual constraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- sum(h) = n_points / 2\n\nThe GA uses tournament selection for parents, uniform crossover for recombination, and Gaussian mutation with rescaling to maintain feasibility. All operations preserve constraint satisfaction through post-processing.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInputs: seed, budget_s, initial_h_values (optional)\nSteps:\na. Validate parent solution: if initial_h_values is provided, use it as seed for population\nb. Generate initial population:\n   i. For each individual, randomly select n_ones = n_points//2 positions\n   ii. Set h[i] = 1 for these positions, 0 elsewhere\n   iii. Rescale to ensure sum(h) = n_points/2\nc. Define fitness function:\n   def evaluate(h):\n       corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n       max_corr = np.max(corr * dx)\n       return max_corr\nd. Evolution loop (until time expired):\n   i. Select parents via tournament selection (size 5, 2 winners)\n   ii. Crossover: uniform crossover between parents to create offspring\n   iii. Mutation: apply Gaussian perturbation to 10% of elements, then rescale\n   iv. Enforce constraints by clipping to [0,1] and rescaling sum\n   v. Evaluate offspring fitness\n   vi. Select survivors based on fitness using elitism (keep top 20%)\n   vii. Track best solution found\ne. Termination: return best solution after time budget expires\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault settings:\n- Population size: 50\n- Tournament size: 5\n- Mutation rate: 0.1\n- Crossover probability: 0.8\n- Elitism rate: 0.2\nVariations:\n- Adaptive mutation: increase mutation rate if stagnation detected\n- Multi-population: run parallel GAs with different initializations\n- Hybrid: switch to local optimization after GA convergence\n- Variable resolution: test n_points = 200, 250, 300 with corresponding n_ones = 100, 125, 150\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime allocation: 500s for GA evolution, 100s for post-processing\n- Population size: O(50) individuals\n- Evolution steps: 200 generations\n- Fitness evaluation: O(200 * 50 * (2n_points)) operations\nFor n_points=250: ~ 200 * 50 * (2*250) = 5,000,000 operations\n- Mutation/rescaling: O(n_points) per individual\n- Memory: ~100 * 250 * 8 bytes = 200KB\nRobustness measures:\n- Automatic clipping and rescaling ensures constraints\n- Fitness evaluation handles non-convexity through random variations\n- Fallback to parent solution if GA stalls\n- Early termination if no improvement in 20 generations\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- All values in [0,1] via clipping\n- sum(h) \u2248 n_points/2 via rescaling\nObjective consistency:\n- Re-evaluate using np.correlate(...) * dx\nSanity checks:\n- Confirm correlation array length is 2n_points - 1\n- Verify dx = 2.0 / n_points\n- Ensure maximum value corresponds to correct lag\nReturn contract:\n- h_values: numpy array of floats\n- c5_bound: float value of max correlation\n- n_points: integer",
